@@ -69,6 +69,25 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+Rich can pretty print a JSON document supplied as a string with :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json`. The output is valid JSON, indented by 4 spaces by default (set ``indent`` to change this), and syntax highlighted unless you pass ``highlight=False``::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+You can also log a JSON renderable directly. The :class:`~rich.json.JSON` class is a renderable, so it can be passed to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` or :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`. Long lines are not wrapped by the console.
+
+To use the function without a console, import ``print_json`` from the package root. It prints using the global console::
+
+    from rich import print_json
+
+    print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+Rich also has a command-line entry point which pretty prints a JSON file::
+
+    python -m rich.json FILE
+
